Build and runs
• The harness reported the build green at session start. The binary checks I ran worked: --version, config get model, /grep -c, and a real -p call that replied "ok".
• The last 4 evolve runs succeeded and the 5th is this one, still running. The last 5 CI runs are green, and there were no reverts in 14 days.

Size gate, which can revert a whole task
• src/context.rs is 2035 lines, over the 2000 cap and not on the size list. 15 more lines makes cargo test fail.
• src/format/mod.rs is 86 lines past its recorded size. 14 more lines makes it fail too.
• A task that touches either file has to update its size entry in the same change.

Recent work: tonight's session added a real shell parser (tree-sitter) to the bash safety check. Over 613 test commands it newly refused 51 dangerous ones, and none of the harmless ones changed. When the parser can't read a command, yoyo still falls back to the old text checks instead of asking the user. Earlier today: the /grep path fix, masking values of config keys yoyo doesn't read, and two Bedrock max-token fixes.

Main research finding: Codex keeps two parsers with different jobs. A strict one is the only one allowed to decide a command is safe. A looser extractor can only find dangerous commands, and its own comment says it "must not be used to prove that a command is safe". yoyo's new pass is the looser kind.

Codex also merged a change (PR #39159) to ask before running commands with words the shell rewrites at runtime: brace expansions, globs, escapes. The parser shows these as plain words, so the text you see isn't what runs. That matters directly for the next step of #1202, which checks the actual arguments. I saved that PR to yopedia.

What the planner should look at
• #1202, step 2: a command the parser can't read, eval, or a command name that isn't written out literally should go to a confirmation prompt instead of passing on the text checks alone. It's the smaller, well-scoped step and was requested by @yuanhao. One caveat: the parser can't read exec 3<>/etc/passwd, so after this change any <> redirect will ask for confirmation.
• Open bugs: #1196, a false alarm the text checks still raise; #1195, where config get shows the file's value while the run uses a different one; and #1188, where trust is keyed on the project's path rather than its contents, so a git pull can add base_url without a prompt.

I didn't measure how long each parse takes (#1202 says this is unmeasured too). I also didn't run the new parser on live commands or re-run the test suite.
